#1  Complex numbers
===============================================================================================

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_number
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argument_(complex_analysis)
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartesian_coordinate_system
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_plane
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythagorean_theorem
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trigonometry
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trigonometric_functions
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverse_trigonometric_functions

  A complex number is the sum of a real number and an imaginary number.
  It takes the form:

    z  =  x  +  √(-1) * y                //  √(x) means "square root of x".

    where x is the real part of z:      Re (z)  =  x
    and y is the imaginary part of z:   Im (z)  =  y

  Let's imagine a Cartesian plane whose horizontal axis (abscissa) is the ensemble ℝ (real numbers) and whose vertical axis (ordinate)
  is the ensemble I (pure imaginary numbers). This plane is called the complex plane.

  Now imagine we plot z on this plane. It shows how any complex number can be regarded as a vector joining the origin of both axis and the
  point with coordinates {x = Re (z) , y = Im (z)}.

  This vector is the diagonal of a rectangle with two sides = Re (z) and two sides = Im (z).
  The vector z splits this rectangle in two identical right triangles whose hypotenuse is the length of the vector.

  This hypotenuse is called the absolute value of the complex number z and is notated |z|.

  The theorem of Pythagoras gives:

    |z|  =  √( Re (z)²  +  Im (z)² )

  Let's call θ the angle formed by the vector z and the real (horizontal) axis. θ is the argument of the complex number z and is notated arg (z).
  Since Re (z) is its adjacent side and since Im (z) is its opposite side:

    tan (θ)  =  Im (z) / Re (z)

  And thus:

    arg  (z)  =  arctan ( Im (z) / Re (z) )

  
  We also know that  cos (θ)  =  adjacent / hypotenuse   and    sin (θ)   =  opposite / hypotenuse
  which means:      adjacent  =  cos (θ) * hypotenuse    and    opposite  =  sin (θ) * hypotenuse

  Remember that adjacent is Re (z), opposite is Im (z), hypotenuse is |z| and θ is arg (z).
  Also that  z  =  Re (z)  +  √(-1) * Im (z).

  Then:

    Re (z)  =  |z| * cos ( arg (z) )
    Im (z)  =  |z| * sin ( arg (z) )

  Then:

    z  =  |z| * [ cos ( arg (z) )  +  √(-1) * sin ( arg (z) ) ]

    This expression is called the polar form of the complex number z.

  The absolute value |z| of z is sometimes also called "modulus" or "magnitude".
  The argument arg (z) of z is sometimes also called "phase".



