  All you have to do for the integral to keep representing a real signal is explicitly stating:  A(-ω) = A(ω)  and  φ(-ω) = -φ(ω).
  But if you don't, it's not a mistake per se: it just means that the signal is not purely real.

  This might be clearer:

    bool isReal     = ( A(-ω) == A(ω)  &&  φ(-ω) == -φ(ω) );
    bool isComplex  = !isReal;



